1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:11,527
Snoring is one of the most common sleep problems. And the person lying next to you often loses as much sleep as you do.

2
00:00:11,727 --> 00:00:18,984
In this video, we'll look at why snoring happens, and which fixes you can do at home actually have research behind them.

3
00:00:21,083 --> 00:00:29,248
Let's look inside. When you breathe in through your nose, air flows down behind the soft palate and the back of the tongue, and into the windpipe.

4
00:00:29,448 --> 00:00:33,222
When you're awake, the muscles of your throat hold this airway open.

5
00:00:33,423 --> 00:00:40,692
When you fall asleep, those muscles relax. The soft palate and the tongue sag backward, and the airway gets narrower.

6
00:00:40,893 --> 00:00:49,962
Air has to speed up to get through the gap, and the soft tissue starts to flutter, like a flag in the wind. That vibration is the sound of snoring.

7
00:00:54,462 --> 00:00:57,971
Some things make the airway narrower, or the muscles floppier.

8
00:00:58,171 --> 00:01:03,423
Sleeping on your back lets gravity pull the tongue and soft palate down toward your throat.

9
00:01:03,923 --> 00:01:14,493
Alcohol relaxes the throat muscles even more. In a small sleep-lab study, alcohol before bed made habitual snorers start having pauses in their breathing.

10
00:01:14,993 --> 00:01:17,831
Extra weight adds soft tissue around the airway.

11
00:01:18,330 --> 00:01:23,893
And a blocked nose, or smoking, can swell and irritate the lining of your nose and throat.

12
00:01:25,993 --> 00:01:34,620
So, what helps? The strongest evidence for something you can do at home is, perhaps surprisingly, exercise. For your mouth and throat.

13
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:45,355
In a randomized trial published in 2015, adults who snored did about eight minutes of tongue, soft palate and cheek exercises, three times a day.

14
00:01:45,755 --> 00:02:00,133
After three months, sleep recordings showed their snores per hour had roughly halved, and so had the total power of their snoring. The comparison group, who used nasal strips and breathing exercises, didn't change significantly.

15
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:17,228
A review that pooled nine studies found the same direction: snoring got quieter and less frequent. And a 2025 trial of a hundred and twenty-five people found that six weeks of tongue exercises reduced snoring, as rated by their bed partners.

16
00:02:17,627 --> 00:02:28,425
The studies are small, and not every one found a benefit. But the exercises are free and safe, and they work like physiotherapy: daily practice, for weeks to months.

17
00:02:28,825 --> 00:02:33,949
Our second video walks you through the full routine, with a timer for every exercise.

18
00:02:36,449 --> 00:02:46,307
Next: sleep on your side. In sleep-lab recordings, people who snore without sleep apnea snore less often, and more quietly, when they lie on their side.

19
00:02:46,507 --> 00:02:59,088
A pillow behind your back can help you stay there. Raising the head of the bed slightly may also help: in a study of people with sleep apnea, a gentle tilt cut breathing interruptions by about a third.

20
00:03:01,588 --> 00:03:12,637
Go easy on alcohol, especially in the evening. A review of twenty-one studies found that people who drink more have about a twenty-five percent higher risk of sleep apnea.

21
00:03:12,837 --> 00:03:19,004
And if you take sleeping pills or sedatives, ask your doctor whether they could be making your snoring worse.

22
00:03:21,104 --> 00:03:31,462
If you're carrying extra weight, losing some can help. In a small study of heavy snorers, men who lost three kilos or more snored about forty-five percent less.

23
00:03:31,662 --> 00:03:41,960
In a larger study that followed six hundred and ninety adults for four years, losing ten percent of body weight went with about a quarter fewer breathing interruptions in sleep.

24
00:03:42,159 --> 00:03:54,266
Exercise itself seems to help too. In a twelve-week trial, people with sleep apnea who did aerobic and strength training had fewer breathing interruptions, even though they barely lost weight.

25
00:03:56,567 --> 00:04:08,576
Keep your nose clear, and don't smoke. In a survey of over fifteen thousand adults, about one in four smokers snored habitually, compared with one in seven people who had never smoked.

26
00:04:08,776 --> 00:04:18,318
If allergies block your nose, treating them can make breathing easier. Nasal strips do open the nose, but studies haven't shown they reduce snoring much.

27
00:04:20,617 --> 00:04:33,025
Two fun options also have small trials behind them. Playing the didgeridoo for about twenty-five minutes a day reduced daytime sleepiness in people with sleep apnea, and their partners slept better.

28
00:04:33,224 --> 00:04:42,771
And daily singing exercises for three months made snoring less frequent, in a trial of snorers. Both work the muscles of the throat and soft palate.

29
00:04:45,072 --> 00:04:57,685
What about mouth taping? A 2025 review of ten studies found little evidence that it helps, and several studies warned it can be risky, especially if your nose is blocked. We'd skip it.

30
00:05:00,185 --> 00:05:08,749
One important warning. Loud snoring can be a sign of obstructive sleep apnea, where breathing stops again and again during the night.

31
00:05:08,949 --> 00:05:24,677
See a doctor if someone notices you stop breathing, choke or gasp in your sleep, if you're very sleepy during the day, or if you have high blood pressure. Children who snore most nights should be checked too. Sleep apnea is common, and very treatable.

32
00:05:27,377 --> 00:05:40,663
So here's the plan. Do the mouth and throat exercises every day. Sleep on your side. Skip alcohol before bed. Keep your nose clear, and don't smoke. And if you can, move more and lose a little weight.

33
00:05:40,862 --> 00:05:48,213
Give it a few weeks, and measure it. Snore Log can record your nights and compare them, so you can see what actually works for you.
